      "writtenOn": "2025-01-21T18:41:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "!!! Danger\n\nThis potentially fetches licenses from the internet. If we need to paste license text into crates for some reason, this should be done from a static resource committed to the android repo.",
      "revId": "e65dc45f4de31540a2fc826d93a418cefec86fc3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fbf3213c_9ca3ea32",
        "filename": "tools/external_crates/crate_tool/src/managed_crate.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 334,
      "author": {
        "id": 1903830
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-21T18:48:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s basically what we\u0027re already doing with patch files.\n\nWe are already fetching crates from the internet; this doesn\u0027t seem significantly different or worse. It also gives us the provenance of the license file.\n\nI\u0027d really like to reduce the number of patch files we are keeping around.\n\nI guess another way to do this would be to add a \"preserve\" block to the config file, indicating \"this file is not present in the crate, but keep it around when regenerating/upgrading\".",
      "parentUuid": "39e8f720_4059c55f",
      "revId": "e65dc45f4de31540a2fc826d93a418cefec86fc3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9b52e131_a00e9e50",
        "filename": "tools/external_crates/crate_tool/src/managed_crate.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 334,
      "author": {
        "id": 1416371
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-21T18:59:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In theory, all the license files should be one of a small number of enumerated licenses. IIRC add3prf.py used to be able to generate those for you based on the Cargo license. Could we do something similar here?\n\nI understand wanting to decrease the patchfile count, I was suggesting having \"APACHE2.0\" and \"MIT\", etc. packaged somewhere in the development tools, and just fetching from there rather than the internet.",
      "parentUuid": "fbf3213c_9ca3ea32",
      "revId": "e65dc45f4de31540a2fc826d93a418cefec86fc3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
